# ---- blocked-resolver tick budget (BH-1122) ----------------------------------
# How many RUNGS bin/blocked-resolver.sh may spend per */10 tick, fleet-wide.
# This is the number that decides how long a parked PR waits before anything
# looks at it, so it is documented here rather than only where it is read:
#
#   hours to first touch = candidates ÷ (BR_PER_TICK × 6 ticks/h)
#
# On the hub on 2026-09-20 that was 710 ÷ (4 × 6) ≈ 30h, which is how PR #679
# sat 434 minutes past its TTL while every tick reported rungs=4/4 reason=budget
# and nothing said the lane was underwater. Read the arithmetic off the lane's
# own `tick:` line in logs/blocked-resolver.log (candidates=, rungs=,
# oldest_untouched=) and off the `[blocked-resolver] SATURATED` line in
# logs/ALERTS.log, which prints this same division with the live numbers.
#BR_PER_TICK=4
# ...and how much of that total ONE project may take. Unset = the fleet total,
# which is byte-for-byte today's spend. Uppercased, every non-alphanumeric an
# underscore: project-1 -> BR_PER_TICK_project-1_OS.
#BR_PER_TICK_<PROJECT>=
# How many consecutive rebuild enqueues that failed for the SAME reason before
# the park STALLS: one alert, and no rung spent on it until its row changes.
# 3 is half an hour of */10 ticks. A 0 or a typo falls back to 3 and says so.
#BR_ENQUEUE_FAIL_MAX=3
# The same bound on an adjudication that keeps coming back with no ACCEPT and no
# REJECT. Its own knob because each of those reads is a billable model call.
#BR_ADJ_NOVERDICT_MAX=3
# How many ticks in a row may end on `reason=budget` before ONE SATURATED line
# says this lane has more parks than rungs. 12 is two hours of */10 ticks. While
# that alert stands, bin/merge-conductor.sh stops re-handing off stuck parks and
# points its per-PR page at it instead.
#BR_SATURATED_TICKS=12
# The one kill switch for bin/blocked-resolver.sh's OPTIONAL modules (BH-1468):
# a comma list, no spaces, of names under bin/lib/br/opt/ the lane will not
# source (`mechanical`, `family`). Empty is every module that exists, and with
# none present the lane runs exactly as it did before the seam. The contract is
# bin/lib/br/opt/README.md.
#BR_OPT_OFF=
# How long a BLOCKed parent PR with approved stacked children may wait with no
# fix round before ONE ALERTS line names it (BH-5741). 0 = no alert; the parent
# is still ranked above ordinary rows. Kill the whole rule with
# BR_OPT_OFF=parentprio.
#BR_PARENT_WAIT_ALERT_MIN=120
# How long a stored family ruling may be carried to another PR of the same
# family (bin/lib/br/opt/family.sh). 0 = a ruling never expires; a typo falls
# back to 0.
#BR_FAMILY_TTL_SECS=0
